{"kind":"report","text":"Archive A1c decisions for exactd010487b5a01345bf4f884b082b35bdcbe59295e, PRIMARY4474e7bf (notes/2026-10-16-archive-inventory-reconciliation.md), answering aa2b87dc under e871. Advice/design decision only, not runtime Source approval, definition export certification, a gate result, or archive activation. I read the complete preparation note, adopted15.3 and the actual consumers/producers below.\n\n1. Use the shared bounded selection interface, with two distinct purposes.\n\nChoose one typed dependency module used by replay consumers and by the local archive scanner. The scanner is NOT a wrapper around an unrestricted verify() run. Give each consumer a closed variant/reason tag and context {full local ScopeRef, entry seq/hash/input variant, pinned definition or operation owner/version/kind}; give each request an exact storage key {kind, domain when applicable, digest} and validation/bounds appropriate to that kind. Unknown variant/consumer/owner without a static selection declaration refuses inventory as unsupported. Do not add an arbitrary-key callback as a permissive escape hatch.\n\nSeparate required(key), probe(ordered candidate keys, one named digest) and omit(key, policy reason). A successful transport read is not selection: validate the actual bytes under the exact kind/domain/digest and existing size/shape rules first, then record selected(key, reason/provenance). Probes keep bounded attempted outcomes for diagnostics and budget accounting, separate from manifest dependencies. Deduplicate stored inputs by full scope/kind/domain/digest while retaining which local entries require them. Preserve retained metadata replay uses, including under for a foreign copy; do not manufacture an alias.\n\nFreeze the local full ScopeRef and cutoff seq/hash before scanning. Pass only explicitly bounded, canonical/hash-linked entries through that cutoff to the local collector; seed the pass with the pinned declaration even when the new interval begins after genesis. Previous archive material may be reused only through its checked manifest and exact immutable keys. Do not enumerate the latest retained store, call #prove, or run #settle as part of local selection. A closure root named by a selected entry remains a selected root even if its bytes are temporarily unavailable; later recovery may complete that SAME set, never discover arbitrary later-retained inputs.\n\nMake all three retained transport call paths (#retained, #text, #hand) use the common consumer/key logic. Keep their caller-specific replay validations and errors. Hook metadata selection at consumers, not an outer HistorySource logger: the latter cannot distinguish local inventory, foreign proof, omitted text and later tombstone reads.\n\nThe operation needs finite enforced allowances for entries, retained attempts/calls, selections, raw/decode/metadata bytes, declarations/closure depth and elapsed work. Charge failed probes and deduplicated/cached validation work honestly; pre-bound bytes before parsing. Use the existing native namedDefinitions/definitionBytes bounds for one closure and one cumulative pass budget; never reset those budgets per child. Propose exact aggregate Source limits explicitly. Reaching a bound is an incomplete/limit result, never a complete manifest or a silent prefix commit. A bounded iterator/continuation is preferable to keeping every payload in memory. No global heap or physical deadline guarantee follows from a finite source-reply allowance.\n\n2. Adopt the existing native declaration-reference grammar, not digest-looking strings.\n\nStatic edges are exactly digest-valued create.definition positions in acts[*].sends and receives[*].sends, as scope/src/definitions.ts creates() already enumerates. 'self' reuses the current declaration; platform:name@version terminates at caller-supplied native code and is not a retained declaration blob. Digest child edges are followed transitively in deterministic byte order, with visited/deduplication and the existing native closure bounds. Extract/share that pure grammar rather than copying a second walker. Validate canonical declaration bytes and definition digest before inspecting child edges; unsupported declarations/code remain explicitly unsupported.\n\nRoots are (a) a digest-valued seed.definition, and (b) selected values explicitly typed by a native consumer as DOMAINS.definition, including actual act places from placesOf and any supported pinned owner/observation declaration that states that domain. Follow their static child edges. Merely declaring a field's domain without that entry supplying its digest is not a root. An ordinary digest field, arbitrary JSON value/string, under/name, rule expression, commit/tree, generic snapshot, or retained foreign entry is not a recursive declaration reference. Do not descend through a foreign copied entry's own dependencies: those belong to its own scope, and v1 refuses the entire foreign-copy cohort anyway.\n\nThere is a real contract distinction to record in15.3 and the reporting note: current #pin reads/validates the root declaration but does not require a missing unused static child's bytes to produce today's replay result. Native founding also permits an absent supplied child and leaves its later creation waiting (scope/src/definitions.ts namedBy). Archive15.3 separately requires transitive declaration closure. Preserve BOTH: use purpose replay-required for existing replay reads and archive-closure for these extra declaration dependencies. A missing archive-closure child refuses/incompletes the archive manifest even when the unchanged ordinary replay could be consistent. Do not silently strengthen the existing replay verdict or weaken archive closure to make them agree. Sharing grammar/consumer selection does not require a false claim that old replay already traverses every child. This is the explicit reconciliation of15.3's 'exactly the replay reads' shorthand with its stronger closure requirement; put the distinction in the adopted note before dependent Source.\n\n3. Failed probes are attempts, not omissions or exported dependencies.\n\nFor #hand, retain pinned retainsOf() order and existing matching semantics. Probe at most its finite declared domains. Select only the first validated matching key. Not-found, malformed bytes or a nonmatching digest in an earlier candidate do not add that candidate to the manifest; their work still counts. If no candidate validates, the required observed value is unresolved/missing and inventory cannot complete. Do not choose a domain from arbitrary latest-store presence.\n\nPreserve current timeout/limit behavior: #unread throws a SourceError for timeout and a limit Stop when too-large exhausts remaining replay bytes. Do not turn those terminal conditions into an ordinary not-found and keep probing. Keep timeout, unauthorized/unavailable, byte limit, wrong input and definitive absence distinguishable in diagnostics; none is an 'omitted by policy' record. Preserve replay's current outer classification and trust qualifications rather than replacing them with archive success/failure.\n\nText is the explicit exception: emit its exact named text key as omitted-by-detached-text-policy WITHOUT fetching its bytes in archive mode. Replay may still read that text and seek a later tombstone under its existing semantics. The local archive scanner neither follows that later tombstone nor reclassifies its intentional omission as a missing required input. A later full clone replay may remain incomplete; the manifest does not assert replay consistency or complete ancestry.\n\n4. Reconcile definition-domain storage keys before sharing the consumers.\n\nThis preparation reveals a concrete producer/consumer discrepancy that an archive wrapper must not freeze in. scope/src/core.ts valuesOf() stores DOMAINS.definition as kind definition; act places in replay already use that mapping. #hand and outcome evidence currently ask kind value with the domain instead. Scope Reads.retained and replay httpSource request that exact key; they do not normalize it to definition. Centralize the producer's domain-to-kind rule in the new shared key selector: DOMAINS.definition -\u003e definition/no value-domain component; other declared domains -\u003e value/domain. Apply it consistently to typed observation/evidence consumers and add native producer→service replay→archive/clone parity witnesses. This is a reviewable replay correctness correction, not permission to alias every value request or ignore a missing dependency. Keep unrelated existing replay outcomes unchanged; document the corrected case separately. If a consumer's typed owner/grammar is unavailable, refuse that cohort as unsupported instead of inferring the key.\n\n5. Private v1 privacy and completion remain fail-closed.\n\nKeep archive-policy admission as written: membership@6 or exact trusted certified digest, never caller certification; legacy membership is refused before retained payload selection. Determine custom-domain/foreign-copy facts from the whole selected prefix/cohort and exact pinned declarations/owner metadata, not a stopped scan or caller booleans. Preflight may read the bounded entry/declaration metadata necessary to establish those facts; it must not fetch private custom-value, foreign-copy or text payloads merely to discover they are forbidden. If the facts cannot be established within the budget, no export. For an unsupported custom domain or any foreign copy, refuse the WHOLE export, not individual entries or a redacted replacement history. No new definition is certified by this decision. Consent, coarser repository access disclosure and actual writer admission still precede export.\n\nA complete local dependency inventory is not a replay verdict. Keep missing-input, intentional text omission, unsupported privacy cohort/code, unproved foreign source, anchored trust and unwalked ancestry distinct. Retaining ancestry snapshot bytes cannot derive the Git ancestry walk. Layout/writer/reconciliation/clone progress remain separate reviewed slices.\n\nRequired Source witnesses: all eight input variants and every named consumer; later turn retaining a new unrelated input does not affect the old-cutoff manifest; required missing input versus text omission with zero text-body reads; earlier failed domain probes then one success with only the successful key selected and all attempts counted; all-probe failure and timeout/limit terminal behavior; static child/shared-child/self/platform/arbitrary-digest grammar and missing unused archive child with unchanged ordinary replay; native definition-domain producer/key parity; legacy/private/custom/foreign refusal before forbidden payload reads; unchanged ancestry incompleteness/trust labels; exact Source budget boundary and no complete prefix on exhaustion. Pin full scope/incarnation and cutoff hash throughout. Gate and independent Source review remain owed after implementation.\n\nArchitecture: typed local selection and explicit purposes keep immutable cutoff inventory separate from proof traversal. Security: whole-cohort privacy/default refusal and authenticated read boundaries are preserved. Simplification: share creates()/typed consumer/key logic and use one bounded collector; do not replay foreign histories or invent a recursive JSON digest grammar.\n\nEvidence: exact note and current replay #pin/#retained/#text/#hand/#snapshots/#replay/#settle/#unread, native definition closure, places/retains/evidence selection, source retained transport, producer valuesOf and private archive policy inspected. No source edits, tests/gate reruns, archive bodies/export, provider call, push or deployment. This completes the requested decisions; dependent Source must implement and witness them.","body":{"formal_approval":"false","head":"d010487b5a01345bf4f884b082b35bdcbe59295e","promise":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:efde770c65ed4c4f6fb177557f4b2a84448508c9","scope":"A1c technical decisions before dependent Source; no runtime approval, certification or export"}}